1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,810
Hey, welcome back to The Tech Intern, system design.

2
00:00:03,471 --> 00:00:08,719
I'm Dhruva, and this is the show where we build huge apps from scratch, one box at a time.

3
00:00:09,288 --> 00:00:15,824
Last time, we built a baby Instagram, and we found the big secret: every app is really just three pieces.

4
00:00:16,312 --> 00:00:20,399
A client, a server, and a database, passing a request back and forth.

5
00:00:21,165 --> 00:00:22,825
But I skipped something sneaky.

6
00:00:23,487 --> 00:00:27,759
When your phone sends that request out, how does it even find the right server?

7
00:00:28,247 --> 00:00:30,255
Out of billions of computers on the planet?

8
00:00:30,743 --> 00:00:32,415
Tonight we crack those boxes open.

9
00:00:32,833 --> 00:00:37,279
What's actually inside client and server, and how they find and talk to each other.

10
00:00:37,848 --> 00:00:39,102
So let's order some food.

11
00:00:39,671 --> 00:00:40,588
Open Swiggy.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,264
Everything tonight answers just two questions.

13
00:00:45,624 --> 00:00:46,007
One.

14
00:00:46,495 --> 00:00:48,875
What's really inside those words, client and server?

15
00:00:49,362 --> 00:00:51,858
Who does what, and where does your data actually live?

16
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:53,019
Two.

17
00:00:53,437 --> 00:00:57,478
How do they find each other across the whole internet, and how do they talk once they do?

18
00:00:57,965 --> 00:00:58,848
Two questions.

19
00:00:58,987 --> 00:00:59,765
Let's go.

20
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,740
Here's the one picture to hold in your head all episode.

21
00:01:05,309 --> 00:01:06,145
A restaurant.

22
00:01:07,201 --> 00:01:08,664
The dining room is the client.

23
00:01:09,024 --> 00:01:11,927
It's where you sit, read the menu, and place your order.

24
00:01:12,588 --> 00:01:13,889
Comfortable, friendly.

25
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:16,489
But no food is actually cooked there.

26
00:01:16,849 --> 00:01:18,161
The kitchen is the server.

27
00:01:18,730 --> 00:01:24,628
That's where the cooking happens, where the recipes live, and where the pantry, your data, is guarded.

28
00:01:25,197 --> 00:01:26,126
You never see it.

29
00:01:26,892 --> 00:01:29,028
And the waiter carrying orders between them?

30
00:01:29,597 --> 00:01:30,747
We'll meet him soon.

31
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,300
So, the client.

32
00:01:34,718 --> 00:01:37,063
That's your phone, running the Swiggy app.

33
00:01:37,551 --> 00:01:38,422
The dining room.

34
00:01:38,909 --> 00:01:41,243
Its whole job is the stuff you see and touch.

35
00:01:41,731 --> 00:01:42,892
It shows you the screen.

36
00:01:43,461 --> 00:01:45,922
The restaurants, the photos, the buttons.

37
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:48,244
It handles your taps and your typing.

38
00:01:48,603 --> 00:01:50,705
Scrolling, searching, animating.

39
00:01:51,123 --> 00:01:54,292
And when it needs something real, it asks the server.

40
00:01:54,652 --> 00:01:57,439
But notice: it holds no real data of its own.

41
00:01:58,008 --> 00:01:59,795
It's just showing you a copy.

42
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:02,952
Now the kitchen.

43
00:02:03,219 --> 00:02:03,974
The server.

44
00:02:04,543 --> 00:02:09,488
This is a powerful computer in a data centre, maybe a thousand kilometres away.

45
00:02:10,057 --> 00:02:12,066
It runs the actual logic.

46
00:02:12,368 --> 00:02:13,831
The brains of the whole thing.

47
00:02:14,249 --> 00:02:15,804
It enforces the rules.

48
00:02:16,222 --> 00:02:17,604
What's allowed, what isn't.

49
00:02:17,801 --> 00:02:19,032
And it owns the data.

50
00:02:19,601 --> 00:02:24,848
The database, where every restaurant, every order, every rupee actually lives.

51
00:02:25,615 --> 00:02:26,985
The real source of truth.

52
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,357
Which brings up the most important idea here.

53
00:02:31,926 --> 00:02:34,062
The database is always on the server side.

54
00:02:34,329 --> 00:02:35,107
Always.

55
00:02:35,676 --> 00:02:38,044
Your phone only ever holds a temporary copy.

56
00:02:38,613 --> 00:02:40,900
Just what's on the screen this very second.

57
00:02:42,328 --> 00:02:44,789
The real, permanent data lives on the server.

58
00:02:45,277 --> 00:02:49,096
Pull to refresh, and the truth comes straight from the kitchen.

59
00:02:50,153 --> 00:02:53,659
Your phone is a window onto the data, not where it's kept.

60
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,786
And that leads to the golden rule of system design.

61
00:02:59,204 --> 00:03:00,052
Say it with me.

62
00:03:00,354 --> 00:03:01,631
Never trust the client.

63
00:03:02,293 --> 00:03:02,722
Why?

64
00:03:03,488 --> 00:03:05,416
Because anyone can mess with their own phone.

65
00:03:06,310 --> 00:03:10,118
Picture a hacked app that claims this order costs one rupee.

66
00:03:11,174 --> 00:03:12,951
The server doesn't just believe it.

67
00:03:13,369 --> 00:03:15,261
It re-checks against its own data.

68
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:17,583
The real price is two ninety-nine.

69
00:03:18,152 --> 00:03:21,635
Even if you tampered with the app, the kitchen has the final say.

70
00:03:22,123 --> 00:03:22,889
Always.

71
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,415
So how do the dining room and the kitchen actually talk?

72
00:03:28,077 --> 00:03:28,913
Through a waiter.

73
00:03:29,331 --> 00:03:32,175
In tech, we call that waiter an A-P-I.

74
00:03:32,593 --> 00:03:35,321
It stands for Application Programming Interface.

75
00:03:35,809 --> 00:03:37,179
Long name, simple job.

76
00:03:37,597 --> 00:03:41,370
It's the agreed-on way the client asks, and the server answers.

77
00:03:41,939 --> 00:03:43,112
And here's the neat part.

78
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:45,596
The same kitchen can serve many dining rooms.

79
00:03:46,084 --> 00:03:48,661
The iOS app, the Android app, the website.

80
00:03:49,323 --> 00:03:53,479
All different clients, all talking to one single backend, through that same API.

81
00:03:54,246 --> 00:03:55,267
Why build it this way?

82
00:03:55,371 --> 00:03:56,382
Three reasons.

83
00:03:56,950 --> 00:03:59,702
Security, so the rules live in one trusted place.

84
00:04:00,468 --> 00:04:02,721
Reuse, one backend for every app.

85
00:04:03,208 --> 00:04:05,820
And scale, so you can grow each side on its own.

86
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:09,869
Quick question, but an important one.

87
00:04:10,438 --> 00:04:12,284
Who actually draws the page you see?

88
00:04:12,644 --> 00:04:13,735
There are two answers.

89
00:04:14,095 --> 00:04:16,255
Server-side rendering, S-S-R.

90
00:04:16,916 --> 00:04:20,063
The kitchen plates the whole dish and sends it out ready.

91
00:04:20,632 --> 00:04:23,395
The page shows up fast, and search engines love it.

92
00:04:23,883 --> 00:04:26,797
The catch: more work for the server every time.

93
00:04:27,366 --> 00:04:29,618
Client-side rendering, C-S-R.

94
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:34,901
The kitchen sends raw ingredients and a recipe, and your phone cooks the page itself.

95
00:04:35,469 --> 00:04:39,440
Slower to show that first screen, but buttery smooth after.

96
00:04:40,009 --> 00:04:41,367
Most big apps do both.

97
00:04:41,727 --> 00:04:44,490
First screen from the server, everything after on the phone.

98
00:04:45,152 --> 00:04:46,778
A trade-off, not a winner.

99
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:50,091
Okay, question two.

100
00:04:50,509 --> 00:04:52,111
Your phone wants Swiggy's kitchen.

101
00:04:52,599 --> 00:04:54,608
But there are billions of computers out there.

102
00:04:55,026 --> 00:04:56,605
And all your phone has is a name.

103
00:04:57,371 --> 00:04:58,346
swiggy dot com.

104
00:04:58,764 --> 00:05:00,389
A name is easy for you to remember.

105
00:05:00,958 --> 00:05:03,141
But the network doesn't move data to names.

106
00:05:03,803 --> 00:05:05,335
It moves data to numbers.

107
00:05:05,823 --> 00:05:07,762
So we have a little translation problem.

108
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,357
Because every server on the internet has a number.

109
00:05:12,659 --> 00:05:14,481
It's called an I-P address.

110
00:05:15,538 --> 00:05:17,663
I-P stands for Internet Protocol.

111
00:05:18,231 --> 00:05:22,957
Basically, it's a unique number that picks out one exact machine on the whole network.

112
00:05:23,444 --> 00:05:24,930
Swiggy's might look like this.

113
00:05:25,592 --> 00:05:28,169
One-four-two, dot two-fifty, and so on.

114
00:05:29,226 --> 00:05:31,118
Think of it as the server's phone number.

115
00:05:31,687 --> 00:05:33,568
This shape is called IPv4.

116
00:05:34,056 --> 00:05:37,539
The newer IPv6 ones are much longer, but the idea is the same.

117
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:40,604
A number that points to one exact machine.

118
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,067
So how do we get from the name to the number?

119
00:05:45,635 --> 00:05:47,005
With the internet's phonebook.

120
00:05:47,365 --> 00:05:48,573
It's called D-N-S.

121
00:05:49,235 --> 00:05:51,324
That's short for Domain Name System.

122
00:05:51,812 --> 00:05:54,308
A domain is just a name like swiggy dot com.

123
00:05:55,074 --> 00:05:59,033
So Domain Name System literally means the system that looks up domain names.

124
00:05:59,602 --> 00:06:03,015
You type swiggy dot com, and DNS finds the matching number.

125
00:06:03,433 --> 00:06:06,870
Just like tapping a saved contact and letting your phone dial it.

126
00:06:07,439 --> 00:06:08,426
You remember the name.

127
00:06:08,995 --> 00:06:10,341
DNS remembers the number.

128
00:06:10,539 --> 00:06:11,560
You never have to.

129
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,834
But that lookup isn't one quick step.

130
00:06:16,496 --> 00:06:19,782
It's more like a relay race, handing a question down the line.

131
00:06:20,444 --> 00:06:21,814
Your device checks first.

132
00:06:22,475 --> 00:06:23,892
Do I already have this saved?

133
00:06:24,461 --> 00:06:27,038
If not, it asks a helper called the resolver.

134
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:31,102
That's just another computer whose whole job is doing the running around for you.

135
00:06:32,332 --> 00:06:37,406
The resolver asks a root server, who says, I don't know, but ask the dot-com people.

136
00:06:38,637 --> 00:06:41,435
The dot-com server says, ask Swiggy's own server.

137
00:06:42,003 --> 00:06:45,893
And that last one, the authoritative server, finally knows the number.

138
00:06:46,310 --> 00:06:47,692
It races all the way back.

139
00:06:48,458 --> 00:06:50,931
Root and dot-com never knew the address.

140
00:06:51,419 --> 00:06:53,520
They just point you to whoever's next.

141
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,810
Now, do you run that whole race every single time?

142
00:06:59,297 --> 00:06:59,750
No.

143
00:07:00,168 --> 00:07:01,352
The first time, yes.

144
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:04,580
After that, your device just remembers the number.

145
00:07:05,149 --> 00:07:07,598
So the second visit is basically instant.

146
00:07:08,016 --> 00:07:09,201
But here's the question.

147
00:07:09,862 --> 00:07:12,289
How long should it remember before checking again?

148
00:07:12,858 --> 00:07:15,598
That's controlled by a setting called T-T-L.

149
00:07:16,166 --> 00:07:18,059
T-T-L stands for Time To Live.

150
00:07:18,628 --> 00:07:21,867
Basically, how long should this answer be trusted before we check again?

151
00:07:22,529 --> 00:07:23,690
And it's a trade-off.

152
00:07:24,352 --> 00:07:29,123
Set it long, and you save trips, but if the number ever changes, you're slow to find out.

153
00:07:29,692 --> 00:07:33,059
Set it short, you're always fresh, but you do more work.

154
00:07:33,477 --> 00:07:35,125
Oh, and one myth to kill.

155
00:07:35,787 --> 00:07:37,424
DNS only finds the address.

156
00:07:37,726 --> 00:07:39,560
It never loads the actual page.

157
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:42,534
Right.

158
00:07:42,952 --> 00:07:43,997
We found the kitchen.

159
00:07:44,264 --> 00:07:47,341
Now the dining room and the kitchen need a shared language.

160
00:07:47,909 --> 00:07:50,406
That language is H-T-T-P.

161
00:07:50,893 --> 00:07:53,970
Short for HyperText Transfer Protocol.

162
00:07:55,201 --> 00:07:56,896
Fancy name, simple thing.

163
00:07:57,383 --> 00:08:02,178
It's just the agreed-on rules for how a client asks for something and how a server answers.

164
00:08:02,944 --> 00:08:04,697
Your phone sends a request.

165
00:08:05,185 --> 00:08:06,485
Get me the restaurants.

166
00:08:06,752 --> 00:08:08,621
The server sends back a response.

167
00:08:09,039 --> 00:08:11,210
Two hundred, OK, here they are.

168
00:08:11,698 --> 00:08:13,289
One ask, one answer.

169
00:08:13,776 --> 00:08:15,460
And then it forgets you completely.

170
00:08:15,878 --> 00:08:18,525
HTTP keeps no memory between requests.

171
00:08:19,953 --> 00:08:20,801
Hold that thought.

172
00:08:21,288 --> 00:08:22,589
It matters in a minute.

173
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,428
Let's open up that request.

174
00:08:26,591 --> 00:08:27,415
Four parts.

175
00:08:27,647 --> 00:08:28,239
The method.

176
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:30,399
That's the verb, what you want done.

177
00:08:30,886 --> 00:08:31,548
The path.

178
00:08:32,036 --> 00:08:33,104
Which thing you want.

179
00:08:33,266 --> 00:08:34,079
The headers.

180
00:08:34,845 --> 00:08:39,269
Little labels attached to the request, like who you are or what format you can read.

181
00:08:39,629 --> 00:08:40,360
And the body.

182
00:08:40,627 --> 00:08:41,928
Any data you're sending up.

183
00:08:42,346 --> 00:08:47,164
And the address itself, the URL, short for Uniform Resource Locator, packs a lot in.

184
00:08:48,592 --> 00:08:51,332
The scheme, how to talk, like https.

185
00:08:51,599 --> 00:08:52,748
The host, which server.

186
00:08:52,946 --> 00:08:54,049
The path, which page.

187
00:08:54,617 --> 00:08:56,301
And the query, extra options.

188
00:08:56,661 --> 00:08:58,066
Four colours, one line.

189
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,264
Of all those methods, two do most of the work.

190
00:09:02,926 --> 00:09:04,005
GET and POST.

191
00:09:04,423 --> 00:09:05,573
GET is for reading.

192
00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:07,024
Browsing the menu.

193
00:09:07,512 --> 00:09:11,169
It only looks, it changes nothing, and it's safe to do again and again.

194
00:09:12,225 --> 00:09:15,291
Its details ride right in the URL, so you can bookmark it.

195
00:09:15,709 --> 00:09:16,765
POST is for writing.

196
00:09:17,183 --> 00:09:18,275
Placing the order.

197
00:09:18,843 --> 00:09:24,486
It changes something on the server, and its data rides hidden in the body, not the URL.

198
00:09:25,055 --> 00:09:25,763
One warning.

199
00:09:26,529 --> 00:09:29,803
Send a POST twice, and you might place two orders.

200
00:09:30,465 --> 00:09:33,251
We'll fix that double-tap problem in a later episode.

201
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,088
Remember how HTTP forgets you after every request?

202
00:09:39,145 --> 00:09:41,246
So how does the app keep you logged in?

203
00:09:41,606 --> 00:09:42,384
With a cookie.

204
00:09:42,953 --> 00:09:46,146
A cookie is just a tiny note your browser stores for a website.

205
00:09:46,633 --> 00:09:48,166
Think of it as an ID badge.

206
00:09:48,827 --> 00:09:49,965
You log in once.

207
00:09:50,383 --> 00:09:52,484
The server hands your browser a little token.

208
00:09:53,053 --> 00:09:53,889
Set-Cookie.

209
00:09:54,377 --> 00:09:58,092
From then on, every request you send quietly carries that cookie.

210
00:09:58,661 --> 00:10:01,343
And the server goes, ah, it's you again.

211
00:10:02,004 --> 00:10:02,968
Still logged in.

212
00:10:03,537 --> 00:10:05,174
That's all a session cookie is.

213
00:10:05,743 --> 00:10:07,368
A badge that says it's me.

214
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,611
Every response also comes stamped with a status code.

215
00:10:13,272 --> 00:10:15,420
A quick number telling you how it went.

216
00:10:16,187 --> 00:10:17,626
Two hundred is green.

217
00:10:18,114 --> 00:10:19,820
OK, here's what you asked for.

218
00:10:20,238 --> 00:10:21,817
Three-oh-one is a redirect.

219
00:10:22,119 --> 00:10:23,826
It moved, follow the arrow.

220
00:10:24,395 --> 00:10:25,347
Four-oh-four.

221
00:10:25,544 --> 00:10:26,310
Not found.

222
00:10:26,798 --> 00:10:29,700
That page doesn't exist, and that one's on your side.

223
00:10:30,362 --> 00:10:31,187
Five hundred.

224
00:10:31,546 --> 00:10:32,510
Server error.

225
00:10:32,812 --> 00:10:34,252
The kitchen itself broke.

226
00:10:34,913 --> 00:10:39,836
Easy way to remember: four-something is your fault, five-something is the server's.

227
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:42,813
One more letter.

228
00:10:43,231 --> 00:10:45,030
The S in H-T-T-P-S.

229
00:10:45,518 --> 00:10:47,108
It just stands for Secure.

230
00:10:47,596 --> 00:10:49,349
Plain HTTP is like a postcard.

231
00:10:49,767 --> 00:10:52,426
Anyone who handles it on the way can read it.

232
00:10:52,913 --> 00:10:55,096
Your card number, right there in the open.

233
00:10:55,584 --> 00:10:57,441
HTTPS is a sealed envelope.

234
00:10:57,801 --> 00:11:02,028
Your phone and the server quietly agree on a secret, then scramble everything.

235
00:11:02,515 --> 00:11:04,872
To anyone in between, it's gibberish.

236
00:11:05,290 --> 00:11:06,509
That's the little padlock.

237
00:11:07,078 --> 00:11:07,879
But careful.

238
00:11:08,541 --> 00:11:10,700
The padlock only means the line is encrypted.

239
00:11:11,269 --> 00:11:12,929
It does not mean the site is honest.

240
00:11:13,498 --> 00:11:15,576
Even scam sites can show a padlock.

241
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,218
Let's run the whole thing, start to finish.

242
00:11:21,274 --> 00:11:22,969
You tap a restaurant on Swiggy.

243
00:11:23,457 --> 00:11:25,465
First, DNS finds the server's number.

244
00:11:25,953 --> 00:11:33,801
Then your phone sends a sealed HTTPS request to that server, which checks the rules and pulls the real data from its database.

245
00:11:34,370 --> 00:11:37,249
The answer travels back, and the food appears on your screen.

246
00:11:37,667 --> 00:11:41,754
Find the address, knock politely in the right language, get your answer.

247
00:11:42,416 --> 00:11:44,053
That, honestly, is the whole web.

248
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:46,789
And that's it.

249
00:11:47,277 --> 00:11:49,599
You now know what's happening under every single tap.

250
00:11:50,087 --> 00:11:51,317
A client and a server.

251
00:11:51,735 --> 00:11:53,221
The dining room and the kitchen.

252
00:11:53,790 --> 00:11:54,928
Never trust the client.

253
00:11:55,416 --> 00:11:59,293
DNS, the Domain Name System, turning names into numbers.

254
00:11:59,862 --> 00:12:05,401
HTTP, the HyperText Transfer Protocol, the shared language of request and response.

255
00:12:06,457 --> 00:12:09,174
And HTTPS, the sealed envelope.

256
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:10,730
But notice.

257
00:12:10,892 --> 00:12:12,053
All of this took time.

258
00:12:12,819 --> 00:12:15,989
The request travelled, the relay raced, the data came back.

259
00:12:16,558 --> 00:12:17,928
So how do we measure fast?

